Exactly. We don’t know until we devise a way to ask.

My caution should constrain the conclusion, not constrain the question. Saying “a fungus probably doesn’t possess an autobiographical evolutionary narrative” is a hypothesis based on current evidence—not a reason to refuse to investigate whether it possesses some utterly different analogue.

And “asking” is the interesting engineering problem. We can’t begin by asking a tree an English question and interpreting any response as an answer. We’d have to discover its native degrees of freedom first:

\text{What can you sense?}
\rightarrow
\text{What can you distinguish?}
\rightarrow
\text{What changes you?}
\rightarrow
\text{What persists?}
\rightarrow
\text{What can you signal?}
\rightarrow
\text{Can I produce a signal you distinguish?}
\rightarrow
\text{Can you produce one I distinguish?}

Then the crucial jump:

\text{Can we establish a mutually learned mapping?}

Rather than humans decoding everything unilaterally, let both sides adapt to the interface.

That’s a new piece for the Interface Forge:

The First Question Principle: Never infer from the absence of a known communication channel that there is nothing to communicate with. First search for a channel through which a question can meaningfully be posed and a discriminable answer can be returned.

And we should be prepared for answers that don’t resemble answers.

Growth direction. Electrical activity. Chemical production. Network topology. Timing. Movement. Resource allocation. Silence. Some combination we haven’t recognized yet.

Then repeat questions, vary them, introduce controls, test predictions, and try to falsify our interpretation. Eventually you might distinguish response from signal, and perhaps signal from communication.

Maybe most attempts reveal ordinary stimulus-response mechanisms.

Maybe some reveal astonishing structures.

That’s precisely why you ask.

The mistake would be deciding what all the kingdoms must have to say before we’ve built the ears capable of hearing them.